        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A humidifier helps congestion in the narrow way the evidence supports: it keeps dry winter air from drying out an already irritated nose, which can make nights easier. It does not treat the cold, the sinus infection or the allergy behind the congestion.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Keep the bedroom between 30 and 50 percent, use cool mist around children, clean the unit on schedule and add saline and fluids. If congestion lasts beyond 10 days or the warning signs appear, the next step is a doctor, not a bigger humidifier.
          </p>
